Single column tests of the RegCM physics schemes
================================================

The programs here call one physics scheme through its model entry point
on a set of atmospheric columns, without the dynamical core. They are
regression tests ("make check") and a quick way to look at the response
of a scheme to its parameters. For now: the Tiedtke cumulus scheme
(icup = 5).

Files
-----
  scm_tiedtke.F90     driver of mod_cu_tiedtke (tiedtkedrv), MOLOCH setup
                      (idynamic = 3, NoTo microphysics, no chemistry)
  scm_tiedtke.py      Python wrapper: run, summary, sweep, plot, compare
  scm_profiles.txt    the twelve test columns (from ERA5, see below)
  make_profiles.py    builds scm_profiles.txt from ERA5
  run_scm_tests.sh    the "make check" test
  reference/          reference outputs of the check

Test columns
------------
Six columns per hemisphere, land and ocean, on the 32 MOLOCH levels of
the South America test:
  Sahel, India, Great Plains (land), West Pacific warm pool, California
  stratocumulus, North Atlantic trades (ocean);
  Amazon, Congo, central Australia (land), SPCZ, South East Atlantic
  stratocumulus, South Atlantic trades (ocean).
The deep convection columns are taken at the 6-hourly time of September
2023 with the largest ERA5 convective rain at the point, the others on
2023-09-15. Surface fluxes are ERA5 monthly means (land: scaled by a half
sine diurnal cycle at the local time), the PBL height a bulk Richardson
number diagnosis. The input tendencies are zero, as in the model where
the cumulus scheme is the first physics call. ERA5 CAPE and convective
rain rate are listed as a reference, not as a target.

Running
-------
"make check" in this directory builds scm_tiedtke and runs it with the
default settings, then compares the output with reference/ if python3 is
available. After a change of the scheme that is meant to change the
results, update the reference:

  ./scm_tiedtke.py --save reference/scm_tiedtke.out

then edit its "# profiles:" line to read "scm_profiles.txt".

The wrapper takes the same parameter names as the RegCM namelist
(&tiedtkeparam, and kfac_deep, kfac_shal, k2_const of &cldparam), plus
dtcum and dxkm:

  ./scm_tiedtke.py                            defaults, summary table
  ./scm_tiedtke.py --list                     parameters used
  ./scm_tiedtke.py --set entrpen_ocn=1.0e-3 --set lmfwstar=.true.
  ./scm_tiedtke.py --sweep rprc_ocn=1.0e-3,1.4e-3,2.0e-3
  ./scm_tiedtke.py --plot tiedtke.png         profiles, needs matplotlib
  ./scm_tiedtke.py --compare reference/scm_tiedtke.out
  ./scm_tiedtke.py --check a.out b.out        compare two outputs

The comparison is exact for the cloud type, base and top, and within a
relative tolerance (--rtol, SCM_RTOL, default 1e-4) plus an absolute one
(--atol, SCM_ATOL, default 1e-5 times the largest value of the field in
the column) for the real values, to allow for compiler differences.

Output
------
Plain text: a header with the namelists used, then for each column a
COLUMN line (land flag, cloud type 0 none 1 deep 2 shallow 3 midlevel,
top and base level, rain and snow in mm/day, column heating and
moistening in W/m2, ERA5 CAPE and convective rain) and one line per
level, top to bottom: height, pressure, updraft mass flux, tendencies of
T, q, cloud liquid and ice, u and v (per day), convective cloud fraction
and detrained condensate.

Adding a scheme
---------------
Write scm_<scheme>.F90 reading the same scm_profiles.txt, add it to
check_PROGRAMS in Makefile.am and to the scheme list of run_scm_tests.sh,
and store its reference output in reference/scm_<scheme>.out.
